.SH NAME
maknoon-serve-identity - Serve identity records via WKD (Web Key Directory) over HTTP


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmaknoon serve-identity [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Starts an HTTP server that serves your Maknoon identity records at:

.PP
GET /.well-known/maknoon/\&.json

.PP
This lets others resolve your public keys without needing a full web server.
Point a reverse proxy (nginx, caddy) at this server for TLS termination.

.PP
The server discovers identities from the keys directory and signs each
response with the corresponding signing key on the fly.

.PP
Example:
  maknoon serve-identity --address :8080
  # Then configure nginx to proxy /.well-known/maknoon/ to :8080


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--address\fP=":8080"
	Listen address

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for serve-identity

.PP
\fB--keys-dir\fP=""
	Keys directory (default: configured KeysDir)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--fips\fP[=false]
	Enforce strict FIPS-140 compliant cryptographic standards

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Output results in JSON format

.PP
\fB--passphrase-fd\fP=0
	Read passphrase from file descriptor N (e.g., --passphrase-fd 3  3<<<$(pass show key)). Also settable via MAKNOON_PASSPHRASE_FD

.PP
\fB--passphrase-file\fP=""
	Read passphrase from a file instead of prompting (use - for stdin). Also settable via MAKNOON_PASSPHRASE_FILE

.PP
\fB--policy\fP=""
	Path to a declarative governance policy file (JSON)

.PP
\fB--tpm\fP[=false]
	Enable TPM 2.0 hardware-backed storage for keys

.PP
\fB--tpm-device\fP="/dev/tpmrm0"
	Path to the TPM device node

.PP
\fB--tpm-pcrs\fP=[]
	List of PCRs to bind keys to (e.g., 7,14)

.PP
\fB--trace\fP[=false]
	Enable low-level diagnostic tracing to stderr


